    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d982">

                <pb facs="1227" n="1209" sameAs="frus:frus1946v11/pg1209"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">823.248/2–1546: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Peru</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Pawley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Lima</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-02-15T16:00:00-05:00">February 15, 1946—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 10:34 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>171. ReDeptairgram 65, February 6. I concur in program of scheduled equipment for
                    Peruvian Army. In full accord with expressed belief this equipment will furnish
                    desirable support for US military mission.</p>
                <p>When combined with interim allocations military aircraft to Peru reDeptel 25
                    January 11, 9 p.m.,<anchor n="12" xml:id="d982-f1"/> implied preliminary
                    commitments to War and Air Ministries made during exploratory staff
                    conversations will have been liquidated. However Dept will recall that Navy
                    Ministry also participated in staff conversations and that General Arnold
                    conferred with Peruvian Navy Minister<anchor n="13" xml:id="d982-f2"/> on same
                    basis as with War and Air Ministers. From Navy Minister General Arnold also
                    received list of interim equipment required by Peruvian Navy.</p>
                <p>US Naval Mission to Peru probably more than any US agency has stimulated sympathy
                    and desire for cooperation with US. Navy mission first US mission assigned to
                    Peru and Peruvian Navy of all Peruvian Armed Forces is most frankly pro-US. I
                    earnestly hope that Dept is formulating list of interim equipment for Peruvian
                    Navy <pb facs="1228" n="1210" xml:id="pg1210"/> in order I may be empowered to
                    assure Navy Minister, at time US Army representatives receive authority to
                    inform Peruvian War Minister of interim army implementation, that interim
                    equipment for Navy will be forthcoming and details will be available at some
                    date in near future.<anchor n="14" xml:id="d982-f3"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Pawley</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d982-f1" n="12" xml:id="d982-f1-content">
                <p>Presumably Department telegram 26, <ref target="#pg1206">p. 1206</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d982-f2" n="13" xml:id="d982-f2-content">
                <p>Rear Adm. José R. Alzamora.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d982-f3" n="14" xml:id="d982-f3-content">
                <p>The Department advised the Ambassador in airgram 90, February 26, 1946, that
                    legislative restrictions prevented the transfer of surplus: armed vessels, but
                    surplus equipment and observation planes were scheduled for disposal to Latin
                    America (823.248/21546).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
